    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-17696",
      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/504024983dafee0fd49c0bb8227f33cc855e2ee9e4bb797f067f0a5e8e415815.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-4f(x-1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-4f(x-1),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/d07f918fae59166e137e47a2c654dfe77ce1c56bb71e931d2d627c509682823e.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/4c237eeb7ecb85ef71c86b73cdbeaae213e74a21604e079f0df4c14c381f6bd7.png)\n\n  \n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-4f(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234522/assets/583f01b214d4c86dcb00936e83f1fad0a4f0f8901b321880d904b2f0da95d7e8.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$",
